00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:11.490
Why do you walk outside?

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:13.240
You know it’s risky, right?

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:15.440
Don’t you value your life?

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:19.539
Or rather, how much do you value your life?

00:00:19.539 --> 00:00:21.169
Could the answer be infinite?

00:00:21.169 --> 00:00:24.130
Could your life be worth everything to you?

00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:28.289
After all, most people would say that they
gain nothing from loosing their life and,

00:00:28.289 --> 00:00:35.809
therefore, the value of their life, to themselves,
must be infinite, but that can’t be true.

00:00:35.809 --> 00:00:40.931
Your life cannot be of infinite value to yourself
because, if that were the case, you would

00:00:40.931 --> 00:00:43.230
never walk outside.

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:45.530
Walking outside is risky.

00:00:45.530 --> 00:00:50.450
Every time you set foot on the sidewalk, you
elevate the chances that imminently, your

00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:52.150
life will end.

00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:55.499
So why do you walk outside?

00:00:55.499 --> 00:00:58.840
Risk is the balance of negatives and positives.

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:04.350
For you or anyone to consider a risk to be
acceptable, the positives must outweigh the

00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:05.450
negatives.

00:01:05.450 --> 00:01:11.180
For example, in a given year, an American
has a one in 55,000 chance of dying by being

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:14.150
hit by a car while walking down the street.

00:01:14.150 --> 00:01:19.080
If you valued your life infinitely, that infinite
value would be multiplied by your chance of

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:25.480
death, 1 in 55,000 per year, to create the
total negative value of walking outside, but

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:28.950
of course, infinity times anything is infinity.

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:35.000
So, you can’t possibly value your life infinitely,
because you do walk outside.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:41.160
You somehow decide that that 1 in 55,000 chance
of death is worth it for whatever you gain

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:43.120
from walking down the street.

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:48.610
This is the process of risk evaluation and,
for absolutely every decision you make, you

00:01:48.610 --> 00:01:51.930
subconsciously balance the negatives and positives.

00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:57.830
Let’s ask another question: would you, personally,
drive a car?

00:01:57.830 --> 00:01:59.820
Most every person would answer yes.

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:04.090
We even know how many people would answer
yes because two thirds of all Americans have

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:05.700
a driver’s license.

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:10.280
So, follow-up question: would you, personally,
go mountain biking.

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:16.230
Would you consider that to be too risky—to
bring you a little too close to death?

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:21.520
Not all, and probably not most, but many more
would answer no.

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:26.190
This is something that has come up time and
time again as mountain biking has gained popularity

00:02:26.190 --> 00:02:31.050
and schools have tried to add it to their
roster of sports, but parents and administrators

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:32.780
said no.

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:37.700
They said that it was far too dangerous of
a sport, that the risk of injury or death

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:38.720
was unacceptable.

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:44.440
However, in a given year, the odds of death
while driving for a given licensed driver

00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:48.020
in the US is 1 in 600.

00:02:48.020 --> 00:02:52.490
Meanwhile, the odds of death while mountain
biking for a given mountain biker is about

00:02:52.490 --> 00:02:54.590
1 in 30,000.

00:02:54.590 --> 00:02:57.470
Is that what would would have expected?

00:02:57.470 --> 00:03:02.330
Prior to these statistics would you have earnestly
thought that this, something you see every

00:03:02.330 --> 00:03:07.940
day or possibly do every day, is more dangerous
than something that you watch people do online

00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:09.340
in awe.

00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:14.770
Most people would say no, so how are people’s
risk perceptions so wrong?

00:03:14.770 --> 00:03:20.069
What makes driving an acceptable risk to nearly
everyone but mountain biking an unacceptable

00:03:20.069 --> 00:03:21.500
risk to many?

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:27.800
Well, it’s simple: engrained in our brains
are flawed processes for evaluating risk.

00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:33.790
For certain reasons, what we fear will kill
us and what will actually kill us are very,

00:03:33.790 --> 00:03:35.860
very different.

00:03:35.860 --> 00:03:40.850
These are the rules of our flawed human risk
evaluation system.

00:03:40.850 --> 00:03:47.340
Rule 1: Voluntary risks are more acceptable
than involuntary risks.

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:52.480
That is to say, people are far more tolerant
of risks that they can choose to engage in

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:54.070
than those that they can’t.

00:03:54.070 --> 00:03:59.620
That’s why most of the population will happily
drive a car, with a 1 in 600 annual risk of

00:03:59.620 --> 00:04:04.401
death, but if a food preservative was introduced
that had the same level of risk, there would

00:04:04.401 --> 00:04:06.310
be mass outcry.

00:04:06.310 --> 00:04:11.080
In fact, studies have shown that people are
roughly 1,000 times more tolerant of voluntary

00:04:11.080 --> 00:04:13.190
risk than involuntary.

00:04:13.190 --> 00:04:15.590
The perception of choice is powerful.

00:04:15.590 --> 00:04:21.289
That means that if measures are introduced
that make an involuntary risk seem voluntary,

00:04:21.289 --> 00:04:26.139
the risk is more tolerated, even if those
measures have little real effect on the average

00:04:26.139 --> 00:04:27.479
overall risk.

00:04:27.479 --> 00:04:32.610
This gets tricky with activities that are
somewhere in between voluntary and involuntary.

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:37.229
Say, for example, a company is polluting the
air in a town.

00:04:37.229 --> 00:04:41.870
This seems like an involuntary risk—you
can’t, after all, chose to not breath the

00:04:41.870 --> 00:04:46.960
polluted air in—however, consider how communication
can reframe this.

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:51.340
The factory owner might say, “you can always
move away,” or, “buy an air filter,”

00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:54.099
or, “stay inside on bad air days.”

00:04:54.099 --> 00:04:59.139
If you give people ways that seem to control
their personal exposure to an involuntary

00:04:59.139 --> 00:05:03.099
risk, they might start to believe that it
is a voluntary risk.

00:05:03.099 --> 00:05:07.659
Not only does this potentially increase the
tolerance of those exposed to the risk, it

00:05:07.659 --> 00:05:11.740
will also increase the tolerance of the bystanders
not exposed.

00:05:11.740 --> 00:05:16.139
They might think, “those people are choosing
to stay there and not use an air filter and

00:05:16.139 --> 00:05:17.389
go outside.”

00:05:17.389 --> 00:05:22.740
The blame is slowly shifted on the individual,
rather than on the originator of the risk.

00:05:22.740 --> 00:05:27.360
The overall danger from something can stay
exactly the same, but give people a semblance

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:30.970
of control over it and they will tolerate
it.

00:05:30.970 --> 00:05:35.400
Rule 2: Acceptance is inversely proportional
to prevalence.

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:40.780
The more people who are exposed to a given
risk, the less the public will accept it.

00:05:40.780 --> 00:05:46.539
We can accept the huge risk of space travel,
with only a tiny number of people partaking,

00:05:46.539 --> 00:05:50.870
yet we could not accept if air travel had
the same level of risk.

00:05:50.870 --> 00:05:55.639
Both are voluntary activities, but there are
two parallel effects taking place here—fewer

00:05:55.639 --> 00:06:01.099
people will engage in higher risk activities,
and fewer people will tolerate high exposure

00:06:01.099 --> 00:06:02.110
risks.

00:06:02.110 --> 00:06:06.819
This effect is also why the risk tolerance
for things like vaccines, which are distributed

00:06:06.819 --> 00:06:12.089
to hundreds of millions or billions of people,
is so dramatically lower than the risk tolerance

00:06:12.089 --> 00:06:18.249
of experimental medicines, which are distributed
to a small and select number of individuals.

00:06:18.249 --> 00:06:22.082
Rule 3: Disease is a yardstick.

00:06:22.082 --> 00:06:26.159
It’s been shown that average human risk
acceptance roughly correlates to whether an

00:06:26.159 --> 00:06:31.029
activity is more or less deadly than one’s
chance of death from disease.

00:06:31.029 --> 00:06:35.629
What’s possibly happening here is that humans
have accepted the likelihood of their death

00:06:35.629 --> 00:06:41.699
by the hands of disease, so partaking in anything
riskier than disease is unconscionable because

00:06:41.699 --> 00:06:46.460
it then makes it more likely to die from that
other activity than disease.

00:06:46.460 --> 00:06:50.490
It changes ones statistically predetermined
end.

00:06:50.490 --> 00:06:54.740
For example, the researcher who defined this
rule found that the average risk of death

00:06:54.740 --> 00:06:59.479
per hour for a soldier fighting in the Vietnam
war was roughly equal to the average risk

00:06:59.479 --> 00:07:02.399
of dying per hour from disease as a human.

00:07:02.399 --> 00:07:07.599
Therefore, the general public more or less
accepted the risk of those fighting there.

00:07:07.599 --> 00:07:11.680
This disregards the fact that the risk for
the individual soliders fighting in Vietnam

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:16.759
of dying from disease was, at their average
age, dramatically lower than the overall population

00:07:16.759 --> 00:07:20.789
average, which is skewed heavily by those
in old age, but that didn’t matter because

00:07:20.789 --> 00:07:28.319
the public’s yardstick is disease—riskier,
it’s not ok, less risky, it’s ok.

00:07:28.319 --> 00:07:33.080
Rule 4: Novelty increases perceived risk.

00:07:33.080 --> 00:07:36.660
We fear what is new and not yet understood.

00:07:36.660 --> 00:07:41.779
As understanding goes up, perceived risk goes
down, and as understanding goes down, perceived

00:07:41.779 --> 00:07:43.499
risk goes up.

00:07:43.499 --> 00:07:48.360
For example, in one now-famous 1970s study,
college students were asked to rank thirty

00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:52.550
activities and technologies from most to least
risky—simple as that.

00:07:52.550 --> 00:07:58.800
In the end, on average, they ranked nuclear
power as the riskiest of these thirty, above

00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:02.349
cars, handguns, smoking, and everything else.

00:08:02.349 --> 00:08:07.929
Then, experts, whose risk perception was shown
in separate research to closely match real

00:08:07.929 --> 00:08:10.749
risk, ranked the same list of thirty.

00:08:10.749 --> 00:08:16.669
To them, nuclear power was the 20th most risky
activity or technology on the list.

00:08:16.669 --> 00:08:22.029
Meanwhile, non-nuclear electric power was
19th on the college student’s list, but

00:08:22.029 --> 00:08:24.180
9th on the expert’s list.

00:08:24.180 --> 00:08:28.929
The college students overestimated the risk
of nuclear power, and underestimated the risk

00:08:28.929 --> 00:08:30.279
of electric power.

00:08:30.279 --> 00:08:34.430
So what’s the difference between nuclear
and non-nuclear electric power?

00:08:34.430 --> 00:08:39.940
Well, normal electric power had been widespread
for a century at the time when this research

00:08:39.940 --> 00:08:41.479
was done.

00:08:41.479 --> 00:08:45.270
Nuclear power, meanwhile, was just gaining
prevalence.

00:08:45.270 --> 00:08:49.950
This risk perception study was done before
the well-known meltdowns at Three Mile Island

00:08:49.950 --> 00:08:54.670
and Chernobyl, just single-digits of people
had died directly from nuclear energy incidents

00:08:54.670 --> 00:08:59.889
at this time, yet people feared it more than
cars, which killed 50,000 people in the US

00:08:59.889 --> 00:09:02.050
in the year when they were surveyed.

00:09:02.050 --> 00:09:07.640
Today, four decades later, risk perception
of nuclear power is greatly reduced because

00:09:07.640 --> 00:09:09.680
it is no longer new.

00:09:09.680 --> 00:09:13.840
Younger people now learn how nuclear power
works in school, and older people have a longer

00:09:13.840 --> 00:09:17.709
period of evidence for the risk level of nuclear
power.

00:09:17.709 --> 00:09:20.420
Understanding leads to acceptance.

00:09:20.420 --> 00:09:24.089
Rule 5: Numbers are numbing.

00:09:24.089 --> 00:09:30.310
The value of saving one human life should
be half of the value of saving two human lives,

00:09:30.310 --> 00:09:35.240
and then the value of saving two human lives
should be half of saving four human lives,

00:09:35.240 --> 00:09:37.029
and so on and so forth.

00:09:37.029 --> 00:09:41.790
That’s intuitive, that makes sense, each
life is, on average, worth saving as much

00:09:41.790 --> 00:09:46.750
as the next meaning that a graph, with the
number of lives saved on the x axis and value

00:09:46.750 --> 00:09:51.089
of lives saved on the y, should look like
this—a straight line.

00:09:51.089 --> 00:09:55.470
There’s even an argument that, going a level
deeper, the graph should look like this since

00:09:55.470 --> 00:10:00.269
large-scale loss of life has a more widespread
negative societal effect.

00:10:00.269 --> 00:10:04.820
Loosing a million lives would be more than
a million times worse than loosing one life,

00:10:04.820 --> 00:10:05.820
for a society.

00:10:05.820 --> 00:10:10.149
So, the graph should look either like this
or this.

00:10:10.149 --> 00:10:15.250
However, if you change this graph from what
humans logically agree on to what they perceive

00:10:15.250 --> 00:10:19.570
psychologically, research shows it looks like
this.

00:10:19.570 --> 00:10:25.399
Humans perceive that saving the 1000th life
is less worth it than saving the 1st life,

00:10:25.399 --> 00:10:28.660
even though logically, they are of equal value.

00:10:28.660 --> 00:10:33.339
This can partially explain how people can
tolerate mass casualty natural disasters or

00:10:33.339 --> 00:10:39.110
pandemics or genocides with little more concern
than they do small terrorist attacks.

00:10:39.110 --> 00:10:43.910
While this phenomenon doesn’t make logical
sense, it does make some psychological sense

00:10:43.910 --> 00:10:48.540
because it lines up with how people perceive
other quantifiable collections.

00:10:48.540 --> 00:10:53.459
For example, humans value a dollar less the
more they earn—your first dollar is worth

00:10:53.459 --> 00:10:58.470
far more than your millionth, to your mind,
even though a dollar is still worth a dollar.

00:10:58.470 --> 00:11:03.199
In the context of human life, though, this
is more destructive.

00:11:03.199 --> 00:11:08.230
Humans can’t comprehend mass tragedy through
statistics, and so they are not an effective

00:11:08.230 --> 00:11:11.139
means of communication for those purposes.

00:11:11.139 --> 00:11:18.910
It is for this reason why people might underestimate
the risk that causes mass casualty events.

00:11:18.910 --> 00:11:24.699
These rules and others all combine to create
a simple truth: the way we, as a collective,

00:11:24.699 --> 00:11:27.129
perceive risk is wrong.

00:11:27.129 --> 00:11:32.079
We overestimate the danger of low-risk activities,
underestimate the danger of high-risk activities,

00:11:32.079 --> 00:11:37.350
we think things that we like are less risky,
we conflate unfair danger with higher danger—this

00:11:37.350 --> 00:11:42.269
perceptual package works so that perceived
risk swings wildly above and below actual

00:11:42.269 --> 00:11:43.269
risk.

00:11:43.269 --> 00:11:45.089
We just can’t trust ourselves.

00:11:45.089 --> 00:11:51.439
We can think, earnestly, that a risk has increased,
but it hasn’t, or that it’s decreased,

00:11:51.439 --> 00:11:53.220
but it hasn’t.

00:11:53.220 --> 00:11:57.810
Considering that we, the general public, cannot
reasonably know how risky something is is

00:11:57.810 --> 00:12:00.449
exactly why life itself is risky.

00:12:00.449 --> 00:12:05.709
While science and statistics might know the
risk, we don’t, so whether we’re actually

00:12:05.709 --> 00:12:09.220
engaging in acceptable risk is a crapshoot.

00:12:09.220 --> 00:12:12.750
Normally, this isn’t something worth stressing
about.

00:12:12.750 --> 00:12:17.720
Our flawed risk perception systems will average
out, through time, to something similar to

00:12:17.720 --> 00:12:19.150
real risk.

00:12:19.150 --> 00:12:23.470
The only time it’s really worth considering
is when people are making decisions about

00:12:23.470 --> 00:12:26.529
risk that affects others.

00:12:26.529 --> 00:12:33.029
When people, somehow, get to decide what risk
others face, perception is dangerous, because

00:12:33.029 --> 00:12:39.139
it can silently and unknowingly eclipse science,
statistics, and fact.

